.agent-cache/
*.local.md
implementation_plan.md

# ============================================================
#  Generated per-machine state — kept on disk, never published.
#  dfs-bootstrap writes these with {vault_root} RESOLVED to the
#  local absolute path, so committing them would ship one
#  machine's layout as if it were everyone's. Regenerate with
#  dfs-bootstrap instead of tracking them.
#
#  The leading "/" is load-bearing, not cosmetics. Unanchored,
#  `WORKMEMORY/` matches at ANY depth — and git's ignore matching
#  is case-insensitive on Windows, so it silently swallowed the
#  shipped `dfs-bootstrap/templates/workmemory/`. A fresh clone
#  then got a bootstrap.py that exits 2 with "template(s) missing",
#  while every gate stayed green on the author's machine because
#  the ignored files were sitting on disk. Keep this block rooted;
#  tests/test_shipped_assets_tracked.py is the guard.
# ============================================================
/AGENTS.md
/CLAUDE.md
/.agent-rules.md
/.agent-context.md
/.cursorrules
/WORKMEMORY/
/HANDOFF.md

# ============================================================
#  Port-local runtime state
#  routing.json holds YOUR tag→destination table (scripts/README.md);
#  the ledger and transaction journal are per-project working state.
#
#  These stay UNANCHORED on purpose: the consolidator reads its
#  personal routing.json from next to itself (ROUTING_DIR =
#  Path(__file__).parent in scripts/vault-inbox-consolidate.py), so
#  `routing.json` has to match inside scripts/ — anchoring it to
#  `/routing.json` would stop ignoring a real personal config file.
# ============================================================
routing.json
.vault-exec-ledger.json
.vault-tx/

# ============================================================
#  OS / editor
# ============================================================
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
desktop.ini
.idea/
*.swp
*~
